A||||2||WisconsinGT||Vehical has a miss on #4 cylinder||||1||
Z||000000||WisconsinGT||03-28-2001||11:59 PM||||That is what my local Pontiac service dept. told me today. So I have to ask y'all what can be done to correct this problem? Also, what causes this to happen? Can it be a fairly inexpensive repair? Thanks!<P>Oh, It is an 88GT 2.8 engine.||||reg||1
Z||000001||87GTZ34||03-29-2001||10:06 AM||||In the order of cheap and easy to fix:<P>1) spark plug<BR>2) distributer cap<BR>3) plug wires<BR>4) valve adjustment<BR>5) injector<BR>6) head gasket (not likely)<BR>7) valve job  (burned valves)<BR>8) motor job  (worn/broken rings, burned piston)<P>The next step is to take a compression test for all cylinders and make a note of the wear/color of the spark plugs.  This will point you in the direction of the next step.<BR>Hope this helps||||reg||1
Z||000002||Jay in Atlanta||03-29-2001||04:12 PM||||I'd bet it's the plug wires.  This has happened to me on two cars (Camaro and Fiero).  I've also had burnt valves on a 3rd car ('84 Civic).  That wasn't fun.  Good luck!||||reg||1
